  "safety_boundary": [
    "Mutable references require an exclusive borrow of the explicitly supplied owning DynamicArray. The cursor itself remains Copy and detached, so multiple cursors cannot create simultaneous mutable references through safe Rust.",
    "Raw mutable pointer construction is safe; dereferencing requires unsafe caller-side lifetime and exclusive-access guarantees. Focused Miri executes a valid pointer mutation with a live owner and no competing access.",
    "Default cursors and mismatched owners return Failure for access; native default arrow returns null without an owner argument. No unchecked reference-returning Deref/Index implementation is claimed.",
    "Rust MUTABLE=true maps to native IsConstant=false; the opposite boolean convention is explicit in bindings. Legacy value() stays read-only even on a mutable facade."
  ],
  "remaining_partial_parity": [
    "Native cached element/block pointers versus Rust re-resolved explicit-owner access after container exchange, move, shrink or address reuse are not proven equivalent. Valid append-stable traversal and captured ends are covered.",
    "Rust PartialOrd across distinct owners returns None to satisfy PartialEq consistency. compare_position preserves native positional comparisons; no newly claimed exact relational-operator parity across owners.",
    "Unsigned index wrapping and extreme signed offsets outside valid native arithmetic domain are safe Rust extensions, not native behavior claims.",
    "No unsafe const-cast mutable access from a truly immutable Rust owner is exposed.",
    "Protected block APIs, borrowed insertion staging, custom element move/copy/drop traces and complete DynamicArray class parity remain open per prior method-tail receipt."
  ],
